WN.lb(7,{"mt_86DyHo9zO3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which sentence is informal?","choices":["Wanna come round later?","Would you like to visit later?","Thank you for your letter."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a chatty word made from two words squashed together.","explain":"“Wanna” is a chatty way to say “want to”, so the sentence is informal."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You are giving a talk to your class. Which opening fits best?","choices":["Good morning, everyone. Today I am going to talk about volcanoes.","Alright guys, so like, volcanoes are cool.","Dear Class, yours sincerely."],"answer":0,"hint":"A talk to the class is a serious job, and it is spoken, not a letter.","explain":"A clear, formal opening suits a class talk. “Guys” and “like” are chatty, and “Dear” and “yours sincerely” belong in letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Is informal English wrong?","choices":["No: it is right for friends and family","Yes: it is always wrong","No: you can use it everywhere, even in a job letter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about who you would write a chatty note to.","explain":"Informal English is right for friends and family. It is just the wrong choice for serious, formal jobs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word would you swap for “grab” in a formal note asking to use a book?","choices":["borrow","nick","snag"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word is polite and not slang?","explain":"“Borrow” is the formal word. “Grab”, “nick” and “snag” are chatty or slang."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Tom wrote {n} letters to the mayor using “u” and “pls”. What should he change?","choices":["Write “you” and “please” in full","Add more exclamation marks","Nothing: text speak is formal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is text speak chatty or formal?","explain":"Text speak is very informal. In all {n} letters to the mayor, Tom should write “you” and “please”.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the best way to end a letter to someone you do not know?","choices":["Yours sincerely","Cheers!","See ya"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which ending sounds like a serious letter?","explain":"“Yours sincerely” is a formal ending. “Cheers” and “See ya” are chatty."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A class writes {n} notes: some to friends, some to the head. What decides formal or informal?","choices":["Who reads it, why, and where","How long the note is","How neat the handwriting is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the reader, the purpose and the place.","explain":"For all {n} notes, the reader, the purpose and the place decide the style. Length and neatness do not.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which pair shows a short form and its full form?","choices":["I’m and I am","I and me","am and was"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which pair has an apostrophe in one of the words?","explain":"“I’m” is the short form of “I am”. The apostrophe shows where the letter a was left out."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the formal way to start a letter to your headteacher?","choices":["Dear Mrs Hill,","Hey Mrs Hill!","Yo Mrs Hill"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which greeting do letters to people you do not know well use?","explain":"Letters to a headteacher start with “Dear”. “Hey” and “Yo” are chatty greetings for friends."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ella writes {n} texts to her best friend. Which style should they be in?","choices":["Informal: chatty words suit a friend","Formal, like a letter to the head","Text speak is never allowed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is reading these texts?","explain":"All {n} texts go to a best friend, so informal, chatty English fits. Formal words would sound stiff.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is the full form of “can’t”?","choices":["cannot","can it","can not ever"],"answer":0,"hint":"The apostrophe shows where letters were left out.","explain":"“Can’t” is short for “cannot”. In a formal letter, write “cannot”."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is the formal way to say “Can I have some?”","choices":["May I please have some?","Can I have some of that stuff, yeah?","CAN I HAVE SOME?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the polite, formal words “May” and “please”.","explain":"“May I please have some?” is formal. More words or capital letters do not make a sentence formal."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A letter to the council has {n} short forms like “don’t”. How can it sound more formal?","choices":["Write them in full, like “do not”","Add some emojis","Use more short forms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Formal writing spells the words out in full.","explain":"Writing all {n} short forms in full, like “do not” for “don’t”, makes the letter sound formal.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["We do not talk to everyone in the same way. With a friend, you might say “Hi! Want to play?” To a headteacher, you might say “Good morning. May I please ask a question?” Both are good English. They suit different people.","Informal English is chatty. We use it with friends and family. It has words like Hey, grab, gonna and See you. It often uses short forms, like can’t and I’m. Text speak like u and pls is very informal.","Formal English is for people we do not know well, or for serious jobs. Think of a letter to the head, a note to a shop or a talk to the class. It uses words like Dear, May I, borrow, please and Thank you. It writes words in full, like cannot and I am.","To choose, ask three questions. Who will read or hear it? Why am I writing or speaking? Where will it be? Then pick the words that fit. Formal words to a friend sound stiff, like a robot. Chatty words to a headteacher sound rude."],"adult":["This topic teaches children that English has more than one register, and that good speakers and writers switch between them. Formal language suits unfamiliar or senior readers and serious purposes. Informal language suits friends, family and relaxed settings. Neither is incorrect in itself.","The lab builds the skill in five steps. Children tap the chatty clues in a note to a teacher, swap word choices until a request to the headteacher is fully formal, then turn a stiff message to a friend back into a chatty one. They open up contractions for a letter to the council, and finally drag four situations into order from most formal to most chatty, including spoken ones.","A common mistake is to think that informal English is simply wrong, or that formal writing just means longer words and more of them. Children also slip text speak such as u and pls into school work, or use letter phrases like Dear and Yours sincerely when speaking to the class. Be careful not to treat contractions as spelling errors: they are correct, just informal.","At home, play a switching game. Say one sentence, such as asking for the salt, as if to a best friend, then to the King. Talk about which words changed and why."]},"familyTip":"Play “Say it two ways”: ask your child to make the same request to a friend and then to a headteacher. Swap roles and see who can spot each formal word.","keyIdea":"We choose our words to suit the reader or listener, the purpose and the place. Informal English is chatty, for friends and family: Hi, grab, want to, See you, short forms like can't. Formal English is for people we do not know well or for serious jobs, like a letter to a headteacher or a talk to the class: Dear, May I please, borrow, Thank you, and full forms like cannot. Neither one is wrong. The skill is picking the one that fits.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Chatty or formal? Ask who will read it, and why.","opts":{"bins":["Informal","Formal"],"items":[["Hey, wanna play?",0],["See ya later!",0],["Can u help pls",0],["Dear Sir or Madam",1],["May I please leave?",1],["Yours sincerely",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagreg","caption":"Flagship lab: spot chatty clues, swap words for the reader, open up short forms, and rank situations from chatty to formal.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6-MYToNZ39":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Tom was exhausted after the race. He lay down on the grass. What does exhausted mean?","choices":["Very tired","Very happy","Very fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did Tom do after the race?","explain":"He lay down after a race, so exhausted means very tired."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The sky was gloomy. Dark clouds hid the sun for {n} days. What does gloomy mean?","choices":["Dark and grey","Bright and sunny","Very windy"],"answer":0,"hint":"The clue is dark clouds hid the sun.","explain":"Dark clouds and no sun make a gloomy sky, so gloomy means dark and grey.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ava thinks famished means famous because the words look alike. What should she do?","choices":["Look at the clue words","Keep her guess","Skip the word"],"answer":0,"hint":"Looking alike is not a clue. What else can help?","explain":"Words that look alike can mean very different things. Ava should look at the words around it to check."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Dan gave his dog {n} treats. The dog was elated and wagged its tail. Which word you know is like elated?","choices":["Happy","Sad","Sleepy"],"answer":0,"hint":"How does a dog feel when it wags its tail?","explain":"A dog that gets treats and wags its tail is very happy. Elated is like happy, only more.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is a good way to check your guess for a new word?","choices":["Put it in the gap and see if it makes sense","See if it starts with the same letter","Guess fast and read on"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try your word in the sentence. What do you listen for?","explain":"Put your guess where the new word was. If the sentence still makes sense, your guess is good."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"Fish swim in the stream.\" What is a stream?","choices":["A small river","A big rock","A kind of fish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where fish live and swim.","explain":"Fish swim in water, so a stream must be water. A stream is a small river."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"Please put the letter in the post box.\" What is the letter here?","choices":["A note you send","A sign like A or B","A kind of box"],"answer":0,"hint":"The clue words are post box.","explain":"You post a note, so here letter means a note you send. Letter can also mean A, B or C."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Raj said, \"The baby mouse is minute.\" Mum said, \"Yes, tiny!\" Which word you know is like minute here?","choices":["Small","Big","Loud"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mum gives a clue. What did she say?","explain":"Mum said tiny, so minute here means very small. Said this way, minute is a new word, not the minute on a clock."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Kim put on her coat, hat and gloves. \"It is chilly today,\" she said. What does chilly mean?","choices":["Cold","Hot","Wet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why do people wear a coat, hat and gloves?","explain":"You wear a coat, hat and gloves when it is cold. Chilly means cold."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mia read: \"The puppy was soaked after it jumped in the pond.\" What does soaked mean?","choices":["Very wet","Very cold","Very dirty"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to a puppy when it jumps in a pond?","explain":"The clue is jumped in the pond. A puppy that jumps in water gets very wet, so soaked means very wet."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Leo found {n} gigantic pumpkins. Each one was too heavy to lift! What does gigantic mean?","choices":["Really big","Really light","Really old"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the clue: what could Leo not do with each pumpkin?","explain":"Too heavy to lift is the clue. Gigantic means really big, like giant.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You read a word you do not know. What should you ask?","choices":["What does this word mean?","How many letters does it have?","What colour is the book?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which question helps you understand the sentence?","explain":"Ask what the word means. Then look for clues in the picture and the words around it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Grandma said the cookies were scrumptious. Ben ate {n} of them and asked for more! What does scrumptious mean?","choices":["Very tasty","Very small","Very old"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why would Ben ask for more cookies?","explain":"Ben ate lots and asked for more, so the cookies must be very tasty. Scrumptious means very tasty.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"\"I heard the phone ring.\" What does ring mean here?","choices":["A sound the phone makes","A thing you wear on a finger","A circle you draw"],"answer":0,"hint":"The clue words are heard and phone.","explain":"You hear a phone ring, so here ring is a sound. The same word can also mean a ring for your finger."}],"explain":{"kid":["Sometimes you read a word you do not know. Stop! Ask: what does this word mean?","Look for clues. The picture can help. The words next to it can help too. Sam was famished. He ate three bowls of soup! Soup is a clue. Famished means very hungry.","Test your guess. Put your word in the gap. Does the sentence still make sense? Then your guess is good. Join the new word to a word you know. Enormous is like big, only more. It means really, really big.","A word you know can mean a new thing. A bat can fly, or you can hit a ball with a bat. The other words tell you which one."],"adult":["This topic runs from about age 5 to 11. Children ask and answer questions about unknown words, use pictures and the words around a word (context) to work out a meaning, and explain the new word by linking it to one they know. It sits behind reading comprehension: a child who decodes a word but skips its meaning loses the sense of the sentence.","The habit to model is thinking aloud while you read together. Pause at a new word and say: \"Hmm, what does gigantic mean? Let's look for clues.\" Point to the picture, reread the sentence, try your guess in the gap, then link it: \"Gigantic is like big, but even bigger.\" Talking about words like this, rather than just giving a definition, is how most new words stick.","Common mistakes: skipping the word and reading on; guessing from what it looks or sounds like (famished is not famous, scalding is not scolding); choosing a meaning that does not fit the rest of the sentence; and assuming a familiar word keeps its usual meaning (a duck's bill is its beak). Be careful too: clues often give a rough meaning, not an exact one, so check tricky words in a children's dictionary together.","In the lab your child stops to ask about \"enormous\" and finds the picture clue, taps the clue words that show \"famished\" means very hungry, swaps guesses into a gap and watches the picture react, links four new words to words they know, and matches pictures to bat and bill in different sentences."]},"familyTip":"At story time, choose one new word on each page. Ask \"What do you think it means? What's your clue?\" Try the guess in the sentence together, then link it to a word your child knows: \"So enormous is like big, only bigger.\"","keyIdea":"When you meet a word you do not know, stop and ask: what does it mean? Look for clues in the picture and in the words around it. Test your guess by putting it in the gap: does the sentence still make sense? Then link the new word to a word you know, like enormous means really, really big. A word you know can also have a new meaning, so check the clues.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"\"Sam was famished. He ate three bowls of soup!\" Which words are clues to what famished means? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Clue","Not a clue"],"items":[["ate",0],["three bowls",0],["soup",0],["Sam",1],["was",1],["He",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnewword","caption":"Flagship lab: stop and ask, find picture and word clues, test guesses in the gap, link new words to known ones, and find new meanings for bat and bill.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oL9s_bufDp":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one does not belong: 🍎 apple, 🍌 banana, 🪑 chair, 🍇 grapes?","choices":["🪑 chair","🍌 banana","🍇 grapes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for what three of them share.","explain":"Apple, banana and grapes are fruit. A chair is not fruit, so it does not belong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is the opposite of big?","choices":["small","huge","tall"],"answer":0,"hint":"Huge means very big. Find the word at the other end.","explain":"Small is the opposite of big. Huge means nearly the same as big."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the opposite of stop?","choices":["go","wait","red"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a green light tell you to do?","explain":"Go is the opposite of stop. Wait is nearly the same as stop, and red just goes with stop."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam sorts {n} cards into Animals and Food. Where does a 🐸 frog go?","choices":["Animals","Food","Both"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a frog something that is alive and moves, or something you eat?","explain":"A frog is an animal. It hops and eats flies, so it goes in Animals.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A strawberry and a fire truck are both red. Can they go in the same group?","choices":["Yes, if the rule is colour","No, never","Only if they are big"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the sorting rule.","explain":"If you sort by colour, they go together. If you sort by kind, they do not. The rule decides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"🐟 fish, 🐙 octopus, 🐳 whale. Why do they go together?","choices":["They all live in the sea","They can all fly","They are all small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where would you find all three?","explain":"All three live in the sea. They cannot fly, and a whale is not small."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Zoe has {n} 🚗 cars, a 🚌 bus and a 🍞 loaf of bread. Which is the odd one out?","choices":["🍞 the bread","🚌 the bus","🚗 a car"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which ones can take you places?","explain":"Cars and a bus take you places. Bread does not, so the bread is the odd one out.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is the opposite of up?","choices":["down","high","sky"],"answer":0,"hint":"Point up. Now point the other way.","explain":"Down is the opposite of up. High is nearly the same as up, and the sky just goes with up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Leo sorts by kind: food or things that go. Where does a 🚕 taxi go?","choices":["Things that go","Food","Yellow things"],"answer":0,"hint":"The rule is kind, not colour.","explain":"A taxi is a thing that goes. Yellow is a colour, but the rule here is kind."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one does not belong: 🐶 dog, 🐱 cat, 🚗 car, 🐰 rabbit?","choices":["🚗 car","🐱 cat","🐰 rabbit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Three of them are animals. Which one is not?","explain":"Dog, cat and rabbit are animals. A car is not an animal, so it is the odd one out."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia has {n} apples and {n} bananas. Which group do they all go in?","choices":["Food","Animals","Toys"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you eat an apple? Can you eat a banana?","explain":"Apples and bananas are both food, so all {=2*n} go in the Food group.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is the opposite of cold?","choices":["hot","cool","ice"],"answer":0,"hint":"An opposite is at the other end. Cool is only a little bit cold.","explain":"Hot is the opposite of cold. Cool means a bit cold, and ice just goes with cold."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"We sort {n} toys by colour. Where does a red ball go?","choices":["With the red things","With the balls","With the blue things"],"answer":0,"hint":"The rule is colour. What colour is the ball?","explain":"When the rule is colour, a red ball goes with the red things, whatever kind of toy it is.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"🧢 hat, 🧤 gloves, 🧣 scarf. Why do they go together?","choices":["You wear them all","They are all red","They are all toys"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you do with a hat, gloves and a scarf?","explain":"You wear all three, so they are clothes. They are not all red, and they are not toys."}],"explain":{"kid":["A dog, a cat and a cow are all animals. So they go in one group. An apple and a banana are food. They go in a new group.","To find the odd one out, look for what the others share. Apple, banana and grapes are fruit. A car is not. So the car is the odd one out.","You can sort the same things in a new way. By colour, a red strawberry goes with a red fire truck. By kind, the strawberry goes with food. The rule picks the groups.","Some words are opposites. Big and small. Hot and cold. Up and down. An opposite is at the other end. Warm is not the opposite of hot. Cold is."],"adult":["This topic is for ages 5 to 8. Sorting words into groups such as animals, food, clothes and vehicles builds the links between words that make vocabulary stick. A child who can say why a lion and a tiger go together, and why a chair does not, is learning the idea of a category, which later supports definitions, science classification and writing.","Two habits matter. First, name the rule before sorting and test every item against it. Second, notice that the same objects can be sorted more than one way: by colour, by size, or by what kind of thing they are. Asking \"Why do these go together?\" makes your child say the rule out loud, which is the real skill.","Common mistakes: grouping by how things look (all red, all round) when the task is about what kind of thing they are; switching rules halfway through a sort; choosing a near-synonym as an opposite (warm for hot, huge for big); and choosing a word that simply goes with the first one (sun for hot, door for open). Be careful with a group reason that fits only some of the items: a good reason fits every one.","In the lab your child drags picture cards into Animals and Food, finds the odd one out in three groups, sorts the same four cards first by colour and then by kind, picks the reason a group belongs together, and matches four pairs of opposites. Every drag can also be done with a big button."]},"familyTip":"At tidy-up time, ask your child to sort toys into two groups and tell you the rule. Then ask for a new rule for the same toys. On walks, play \"What is the opposite?\" with words like up, wet, loud and fast.","keyIdea":"Words can be put into groups. Things of the same kind go together, like animals or food. To sort, pick a rule and check every card against it. Change the rule and the groups change. Some words come in pairs of opposites, like big and small: an opposite is at the other end, not nearly the same.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it an animal or food? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Animals","Food"],"items":[["Horse",0],["Duck",0],["Bee",0],["Bread",1],["Rice",1],["Pear",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsortw","caption":"Flagship lab: drag cards into groups, find the odd one out, sort the same cards by a new rule, say why a group goes together, match opposites.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_WzOyJFKDIu":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In maths, find the difference between {a} and {b}.","choices":["{=a-b}","{=a+b}","they look different"],"answer":0,"hint":"In maths, difference means take away.","explain":"The difference is {a} − {b} = {=a-b}. In maths difference means subtract, not how things look.","params":{"a":[12,20],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"The current in the wire lit the bulb.\" What does current mean here?","choices":["a flow of electric charge","happening now","a flow of water"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sentence is about a wire and a bulb.","explain":"In a circuit, current is a flow of electric charge. Happening now is the everyday meaning."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word belongs to history?","choices":["parliament","perimeter","circuit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word is about who makes a country's laws?","explain":"Parliament is the group that makes a country's laws, a history and citizenship word. Perimeter is maths and circuit is science."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"\"Summarise the story\" asks you to…","choices":["give the main points, short","retell every detail","say if you liked it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think short: which answer keeps only the main events?","explain":"A summary keeps only the main events. Every detail is a retelling, and liking it is an opinion."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A jug holds {n}00 ml of water. Which word names this amount of space?","choices":["volume","mass","loudness"],"answer":0,"hint":"Space taken up is measured in ml.","explain":"{n}00 ml is a volume, the space something takes up. Mass is in grams, and loudness is the other meaning of volume.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is evidence that plants need light?","choices":["A plant in a dark cupboard went pale and weak","Plants are important","I like green plants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence is something seen or measured.","explain":"The pale plant in the dark is a result you can see, so it is evidence. The others are a claim and a feeling."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"The puddle dried up because the water ___ into the air.\" Which word is precise?","choices":["evaporated","went","melted"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means a liquid turning into a gas?","explain":"Evaporated means the water turned into a gas. Went is vague, and melted means a solid turning into a liquid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In {a}/{b}, which number is the numerator?","choices":["{a}","{b}","{=a+b}"],"answer":0,"hint":"The numerator is the top number: how many parts you have.","explain":"In {a}/{b} the numerator is {a}, the top number. {b} is the denominator, the number of equal parts in the whole.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[5,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"\"In a democracy, adults vote in elections to choose who runs the country.\" What is a democracy?","choices":["rule by leaders the people choose","rule by a king","a big city"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the clue: what do the adults do?","explain":"The clue says adults vote to choose who runs the country, so democracy is rule by leaders the people choose."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In maths, what is the product of {a} and {b}?","choices":["{=a*b}","{=a+b}","a thing made in a factory"],"answer":0,"hint":"In maths, product is the answer when you multiply.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}. In maths the product is the answer to a multiplication; {=a+b} would be the sum.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which word belongs to science?","choices":["photosynthesis","numerator","parliament"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word is about how plants make food?","explain":"Photosynthesis is how plants make food from light, a science idea. Numerator is maths and parliament is history."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Contrast cats and dogs\" asks you to…","choices":["say how they are different","say how they are alike","describe one of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Contrast and compare are not the same task.","explain":"Contrast asks for differences, for example cats climb trees but dogs do not. Compare asks for likenesses and differences."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A field is {a} m long and {b} m wide. What is its perimeter?","choices":["{=2*a+2*b} m","{=a*b} m²","{=a+b} m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Perimeter is the distance all the way round, so add all four sides.","explain":"Perimeter = {a} + {b} + {a} + {b} = {=2*a+2*b} m. {=a*b} m² is the area, the space inside.","params":{"a":[10,30],"b":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"\"In a desert habitat, cactus plants store water so they can live with very little rain.\" What does habitat mean?","choices":["the natural home of a living thing","a kind of cactus","the weather in a place"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the clue words: what do the plants do there?","explain":"A habitat is the natural home where a living thing lives, here a desert where cactus plants survive."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every subject has its own words. Maths has numerator and perimeter. Science has photosynthesis and circuit. History has parliament and empire. Each one names an idea from that subject.","Some words you already know mean something new in a subject. At the shop a product is a thing to buy. In maths the product of 6 and 3 is 18, the answer when you multiply. So first ask: which subject am I in?","Meet a new word? Look at the words around it. In a pond ecosystem, every plant and animal depends on the water and on each other. That clue tells you what an ecosystem is. A glossary at the back of the book helps too.","Task words tell you what to do. Contrast means say how things are different. Summarise means give the main points, short. Evidence is a fact that shows something is true. When you write, pick the exact word: the water evaporated, not the water went."],"adult":["This topic is for ages 9 to 11. It covers two kinds of word. Subject words belong to one subject, such as numerator, photosynthesis or parliament. Task words, such as compare, contrast, summarise, evidence and interpret, turn up in every subject. They tell students what a task wants.","Many subject words look familiar but have an exact meaning, so they are easy to misread. Product, difference, volume, current, table and source all have an everyday sense and a subject sense. Students learn new terms best from clues in the text, a glossary and word parts. They also need to use the words in talk and writing, not just spot them.","Common mistakes: reading a subject word with its everyday meaning (difference as how things look, not take away); mixing up close pairs such as area and perimeter, or melting and evaporating; treating contrast and compare as the same task; giving an opinion when evidence is asked for; and using vague words like went or stuff where an exact term exists. Be careful to check that a learner can explain a term, not just spell it.","In the lab learners drag six words onto Maths, Science and History shelves and choose the subject meaning of product, volume and current. They tap the context clue that explains ecosystem, numerator and democracy, match contrast, summarise and evidence to answers that do the job, and drag the precise word into three vague sentences."]},"keyIdea":"Each subject has its own exact words, like numerator in maths, photosynthesis in science and parliament in history. Some everyday words change meaning in a subject: product means the answer to a multiplication in maths. Work out a new subject word from clues nearby or a glossary. Task words such as contrast, summarise and evidence tell you what job to do. Using the precise word says exactly what you mean.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which subject does each word belong to? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Maths","Science","History"],"items":[["denominator",0],["angle",0],["evaporation",1],["magnet",1],["empire",2],["invasion",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdomain","caption":"Flagship lab: shelve subject words, pick a word's subject meaning, hunt context clues, match task words, drop the precise word in a gap.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_1ro8W1cZYn":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a dialect?","choices":["The English of a place or group, with its own words and grammar","English full of spelling mistakes","A language that is not English"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of wee, nowt and y'all.","explain":"A dialect is a variety of English linked to a place or group. It has its own words and its own regular grammar."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"In Yorkshire dialect, \"There's nowt in the fridge, and I've looked {n} times\" means…","choices":["There is nothing in the fridge","There is a lot in the fridge","The fridge is new"],"answer":0,"hint":"Nowt is the opposite of owt, which means anything.","explain":"Nowt means nothing in Yorkshire and other northern English dialects. Looking {n} times found nothing at all.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the difference between dialect and register?","choices":["Dialect is where you are from; register is how formal you are","They mean exactly the same","Register is only for writing; dialect is only for books"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kirsty keeps her home but changes how formal she is.","explain":"Dialect is linked to place or group. Register is how formal you are for the listener and the moment."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"An author writes a farm lad's speech as \"tha mun come up on t' moor\". What does the reader learn?","choices":["Where the lad is from","That the author cannot spell","That the story is set in space"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tha, mun and t' are words from one part of England.","explain":"Tha, mun and t' are Yorkshire dialect, so the reader learns the lad is from the north of England."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ben uses dialect with friends and Standard English in a speech to {n} parents. What is true?","choices":["He is choosing the variety that fits each audience","His dialect is wrong and he is fixing it","He should use the same words with everyone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Neither is wrong. Which fits a formal speech?","explain":"A speech to {n} parents is formal, so Standard English fits. Dialect fits friends. Switching is a skill.","params":{"n":[30,200]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What happens when you translate a character's dialect into Standard English?","choices":["The meaning stays, but the voice and sense of place are lost","Nothing changes","The meaning changes completely"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Kirsty's line after you swapped every Scots word.","explain":"The line becomes clear to any reader, but the character's voice and home are lost. That is why authors keep dialect."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which sentence is Standard English?","choices":["She doesn't like peas.","She don't like peas.","She dinnae like peas."],"answer":0,"hint":"Standard English uses doesn't with she.","explain":"She doesn't is Standard English. She don't and she dinnae are dialect forms with their own rules."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why might a posh character say \"One simply does not eat in the street\"?","choices":["The words show what kind of person she is","They show the story is set long ago","They show she is from Scotland"],"answer":0,"hint":"One simply is very formal and stiff.","explain":"One and simply mark a very formal, upper-class way of speaking, so they tell you who she is."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a story set in Scotland, a mum says, \"Mind the wee bairn.\" What does she mean?","choices":["Watch the small child","Find the lost bear","Leave the wet bin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wee is a Scots word for small. Bairn is a Scots word for a person.","explain":"Wee means small and bairn means child in Scots. Mind means look after or watch."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A character says \"we was\" on page {n} and \"they was\" on page {m}. What does that show?","choices":["Her dialect uses was with every person","The author keeps making mistakes","She changes her mind about grammar"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it random, or the same every time?","explain":"Using was with we and they every time, on page {n} and page {m}, is a regular dialect pattern. Standard English would say we were and they were.","params":{"n":[3,9],"m":[12,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kirsty says \"Aye, ah'm fair puggled\" to her gran but \"I am very tired\" to her new teacher. What is she changing?","choices":["Her variety and register, to fit the listener","Her meaning","Her name"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both lines mean the same thing. Who is she talking to?","explain":"The meaning stays the same: she is tired. She switches from Scots with family to formal Standard English for a teacher."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A play set {n} years ago has a knight say, \"Whither goest thou?\" Why does the writer use these words?","choices":["To show the story is set long ago","To show the knight is from Australia","Because old words are more correct"],"answer":0,"hint":"Thou and whither are not from a place. They are from a time.","explain":"Thou and whither are old forms of English. They tell you the play is set in the past, about {n} years ago, not in a place today.","params":{"n":[400,800]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You are writing a formal letter to the council. Which sentence fits best?","choices":["We were told the park would open in May.","We was told the park would open in May.","Y'all told us the park would open in May."],"answer":0,"hint":"Formal writing usually expects Standard English.","explain":"We were is the Standard English form, which suits a formal letter. We was and y'all are fine in speech and stories but do not fit this register."}],"explain":{"kid":["There is more than one kind of English. A dialect is the English of a place or a group. In Scotland a small child can be a wee bairn. In Yorkshire, nowt means nothing. In the southern USA, y'all means all of you.","Dialects have rules, not just words. Some people say we was and you was. They use was with every person, every time. That is a pattern. Standard English says we were, and it is the variety we use in formal writing.","Register is how formal you are. Most people switch. Kirsty speaks Scots with her gran, chats with a friend, and says Yes, Mr Shah to a new teacher. Each one fits the listener.","Authors use this on purpose. A line in dialect shows where a character is from. Old words like thou and whither show a story set long ago. Posh or stiff words show what kind of person is talking."],"adult":["This topic is for ages 10 to 11. It matches the standard on comparing kinds of English in stories, plays and poems. Learners spot dialect words and dialect grammar. They tell dialect apart from register. They explain what a writer gains by putting speech in dialect or in older English.","Linguists treat a dialect as a full variety with steady grammar. We was, I done it and she don't are common in British and American dialects, and each follows a regular pattern. Standard English is also a dialect. It is the one used in formal writing, exams and most books. Register is separate: anyone can be more or less formal in their own dialect.","Common mistakes: calling dialect bad or lazy English, and mixing up dialect (where you are from) with register (how formal the moment is). Children also think old words like thou come from a place, not a time. Many think a translated line keeps everything. Be careful to praise the switch, not to erase home speech. The goal is to choose the variety that fits.","In the lab your child matches five dialect words to their meanings. Next they sort dialect grammar from Standard English. They pick what Kirsty says to her gran, a London friend and a new teacher. They decide if a story line shows where, when or who. Last, they translate a Scots line and watch the voice bar drop."]},"familyTip":"Collect family words. Ask grandparents or neighbours for words they grew up with that you do not use, like bairn, nowt or y'all, and what they mean. Then read a story aloud with a character who speaks in dialect and ask: what does this tell us about where they live?","keyIdea":"English comes in many varieties. A dialect is the English of a place or group, with its own words and grammar: wee and bairn in Scotland, nowt in Yorkshire, y'all in the southern USA. Dialects follow regular rules, so they are not mistakes. Register is how formal you are for the listener and the moment. Writers use dialect, old forms and register in speech to show where and when a story is set and who a character is.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it about where, or about when? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A place (dialect)","A time (old English)"],"items":[["wee",0],["thou",1],["nowt",0],["whither",1],["y'all",0],["prithee",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdial","caption":"Flagship lab: match dialect words, sort dialect grammar, switch Kirsty's English for three listeners, read what dialogue tells you, translate a Scots line.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_VLu59hpQ4T":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Put these in order from least wet to most wet: wet, soaked, damp.","choices":["damp, wet, soaked","soaked, wet, damp","wet, damp, soaked"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which is only a little bit wet?","explain":"Damp is a little wet, wet is more, and soaked is wet all the way through."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word means a quick look?","choices":["glance","stare","glare"],"answer":0,"hint":"One of these takes only a second.","explain":"Glance is a quick look. Stare is a long look, and glare is an angry look."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The {n} soldiers move in step, knees up, left, right, left. What are they doing?","choices":["marching","tiptoeing","strolling"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big, even steps in time together.","explain":"Marching is big, steady steps in time, like the {n} soldiers. Tiptoeing is quiet, and strolling is slow and relaxed.","params":{"n":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence shows the speaker is most sure?","choices":["I know it is Tuesday.","I think it is Tuesday.","I wonder if it is Tuesday."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which verb leaves no doubt?","explain":"Know is the most sure. Think is fairly sure, and wonder is not sure at all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"It is so hot on the beach that the sand burns your feet. Which word fits best?","choices":["scorching","warm","cool"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sand that burns is more than a bit hot.","explain":"Scorching fits, because it means very, very hot. Warm is only a bit hot."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Dad gently throws {n} beanbags to the baby, soft and short. What does he do?","choices":["tosses them","hurls them","flings them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Soft and short. Which verb is the gentle one?","explain":"He tosses the {n} beanbags. A toss is gentle and short. Hurl and fling are hard throws.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word is the smallest?","choices":["tiny","small","little"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is even smaller than small?","explain":"Tiny is the smallest. Small and little are close, but tiny means very small."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word is the strongest?","choices":["gigantic","big","huge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture each one. Which is the biggest of all?","explain":"Gigantic is the strongest. Big, then huge, then gigantic: each one is bigger than the last."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The baby is asleep, so {n} children ___ past the door. Which word fits best?","choices":["tiptoe","stomp","race"],"answer":0,"hint":"They must not wake the baby. Which way of moving is quiet?","explain":"Tiptoe fits best. The {n} children need quiet steps so the baby stays asleep. Stomping and racing are loud.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia throws the ball as hard and far as she can. Which word fits?","choices":["hurls","tosses","drops"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means a very hard throw?","explain":"Hurls fits. A hurl is a very hard throw. A toss is soft, and a drop is not a throw at all."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The puppy ate all {n} biscuits in big, fast bites. Which word fits best?","choices":["gobbled","nibbled","sipped"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big, fast bites. Is that nibbling or gobbling?","explain":"Gobbled fits. Gobble means eating fast in big bites. Nibble is tiny bites, and you sip a drink.","params":{"n":[3,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Walk, tiptoe, march and sprint all mean moving on your feet. But they are not the same. Tiptoe is soft and slow. Sprint is as fast as you can go.","Some words get stronger and stronger. Warm, hot, scorching. Damp, wet, soaked. Each one is more than the one before.","Toss, throw and hurl all send a ball up in the air. A toss is soft. A hurl is very hard. Nibble and gobble are both eating, but in tiny or big bites.","Pick the word that fits best. A tiny mouse scurries. A giant stomps. The right word helps people see it."],"adult":["Shades of meaning are small differences between words that are close in meaning (near-synonyms). Common Core asks kindergarteners to tell apart verbs for the same general action, such as walk, march, strut and prance, by acting them out. In Grade 1 they compare verbs that differ in manner (look, peek, glance, stare, glare) and adjectives that differ in strength (large, gigantic). In Grade 2 it is closely related verbs (toss, throw, hurl) and adjectives (thin, slender, skinny, scrawny). In Grade 3 it is words for degrees of certainty (knew, believed, suspected, heard, wondered).","There are two kinds of shade. Verbs often differ in manner: how the action is done, how fast, how loud, how hard. Adjectives often differ in intensity: how much of the quality there is. Lining words up from weakest to strongest on a scale, sometimes called a word ladder or semantic gradient, makes the differences visible.","Common mistakes: treating synonyms as exact swaps, so a child writes 'the mouse stomped'; thinking a longer or fancier word is always stronger; mixing up glance (a quick look) with stare (a long look) and glare (an angry look); and reading 'I think' as being as sure as 'I know'. Acting the words out, and checking clues in the sentence such as tiny, hungry or asleep, fixes most of these.","In the lab your child names three walkers by how they move, orders three sets of words on a strength staircase, pulls back a ball to act out toss, throw and hurl, drops the best verb into four sentences, and places know, think and wonder on a sure-o-meter."]},"familyTip":"Play 'Act it out': call out a verb such as walk, then a stronger or different shade such as stomp, tiptoe, stroll or sprint, and act each one together. Then try a ladder at dinner: 'Is this soup warm, hot or scorching?'","keyIdea":"Some words mean nearly the same thing but not quite. These are shades of meaning. Verbs can show different ways of doing an action: tiptoe, march and sprint are all ways to move. Adjectives can be weaker or stronger: big, huge, gigantic. Some verbs show how sure someone is: know, think, wonder. Choosing the exact shade paints a sharper picture.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each word a gentle shade or a strong shade? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🌱 Gentle shade","🔥 Strong shade"],"items":[["nibble",0],["gobble",1],["tiptoe",0],["stomp",1],["toss",0],["hurl",1],["damp",0],["soaked",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshade","caption":"Flagship lab: name the walkers, build strength stairs, toss and hurl a ball, fill gaps and rate how sure.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_F4ZXmOzc7y":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"'The night was calm. Too calm.' The full stop in the middle of a line of verse creates…","choices":["A caesura","Enjambment","Sibilance"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pause inside the line, not at its end.","explain":"A pause in the middle of a line is a caesura. Here it makes the reader stop and sense unease."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Early in a story, a crow lands on the roof of a house where someone later dies. This is…","choices":["Foreshadowing","Juxtaposition","A semantic field"],"answer":0,"hint":"An early hint at a later event.","explain":"The crow is an early hint of death, so it is foreshadowing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the strongest analysis?","choices":["The juxtaposition of the glittering ballroom and the freezing street makes the rich seem cruelly blind.","The writer uses juxtaposition.","The juxtaposition makes the reader want to read on."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the method, the evidence and a specific effect.","explain":"The first names the method, points to the exact contrast and says what it makes the reader think. The others only label or use a vague effect."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A student writes 'the writer uses imagery'. Which is more precise for 'ice', 'frost', 'numb', 'pale' together?","choices":["A semantic field of cold","Imagery","A motif"],"answer":0,"hint":"Choose the narrowest term that fits a cluster of linked words.","explain":"'Imagery' is too broad. Four linked words in one place form a semantic field of cold."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Is 'Sure, she should shout' sibilant?","choices":["Yes: 'sure', 'she', 'should' and 'shout' all start with a sh sound","No: 'sure' does not start with s-h","No: sibilance needs the letter z"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sibilance is about the sound you hear, not the spelling.","explain":"Yes. 'Sure' starts with a sh sound, so all four words hiss. Sibilance is identified by sound, not letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A poem has {n} stanzas, and every one ends with a full stop at the end of its last line. Those last lines are…","choices":["End-stopped","Enjambed","Caesurae"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sentence closes exactly at the line break.","explain":"Each of the {n} stanzas closes on a full stop at the line end, so those lines are end-stopped.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Chapter {n} shows a wedding; the next chapter opens on a funeral. Placing these side by side is…","choices":["Juxtaposition","Foreshadowing","Sibilance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two contrasting things placed together.","explain":"Setting the wedding in chapter {n} right beside a funeral is juxtaposition: the contrast sharpens both.","params":{"n":[2,12]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a poem about a city, the words 'jungle', 'prowl', 'prey' and 'roar' appear. What is this?","choices":["A semantic field of the wild","A motif","Foreshadowing"],"answer":0,"hint":"A group of words in one place that share one theme.","explain":"These four words share one theme (the wild), so they form a semantic field. It makes the city feel dangerous and predatory."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Line {n} of a poem ends with no punctuation and the sentence carries on into the next line. What is this?","choices":["Enjambment","A caesura","An end-stopped line"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sense runs over the line break.","explain":"The sentence runs on from line {n} into the next line: enjambment. A caesura is a pause inside a line; an end-stopped line closes at its end.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which line shows sibilance?","choices":["She sat in the silent shadows.","The proud piper played.","Cold crows called."],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen for repeated hissing sounds: s, sh, z.","explain":"'She sat in the silent shadows' repeats s and sh sounds. The others repeat p and hard c sounds, which is alliteration but not sibilance."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A broken clock appears in chapter 1 and again in {n} later chapters. What is the clock?","choices":["A motif","A caesura","Juxtaposition"],"answer":0,"hint":"An image that keeps coming back across the text.","explain":"It recurs across the novel ({n} more times), so it is a motif, perhaps standing for time running out.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Writers make choices on purpose. Your job is to name each choice with the right word, then say what it does.","Some methods work on words. A semantic field is a group of words with one theme. A kitchen full of words like battlefield, troops and marched feels like a war. Sibilance is a run of hissing sounds, like s and sh. Listen for the sound, not the letter: sure and she both hiss.","Some methods work on lines of poetry. A caesura is a pause in the middle of a line, often a full stop. Enjambment is when a sentence keeps going past the end of a line into the next. A line that stops at its end is end-stopped.","Some methods work across a whole text. Juxtaposition puts two very different things side by side, like a feast next to a hungry child. Foreshadowing drops an early hint of what comes later. A motif is an image that keeps coming back. Then always explain: what does the reader see or feel because of it?"],"adult":["At GCSE, all the exam boards test how well students analyse language, form and structure. Marks for terms come only when the term helps explain an effect. The key terms here are juxtaposition, foreshadowing, semantic field, caesura, enjambment, sibilance and motif. An end-stopped line is the contrast to enjambment.","The fine lines matter. A caesura is a pause inside a line. Enjambment is a sentence that runs over the line break. An end-stopped line stops at the break. Sibilance is a run of hissing sounds, so you find it by ear, not by spelling. It is not the same as a run of b or p sounds. A motif comes back across a whole text, but a semantic field is a cluster of linked words in one place. Foreshadowing points forward in time. Juxtaposition puts two things side by side so they contrast.","Common mistakes: listing devices with no effect, which teachers call feature spotting; vague effects such as 'makes it more interesting'; a broad label like imagery when a sharper term fits; reading a war-like semantic field as a real war; calling any repeated letter sibilance; and mixing up caesura and enjambment. A safe frame is the method, a short quote as evidence, and then the exact effect on the reader.","In the lab you tap the war words in a kitchen scene, tag points in a short poem as caesura, enjambment or end-stopped, sort lines by sibilance, name the method behind three story clues, and build one sentence of analysis. Vague and literal options are turned down."]},"familyTip":"Pick a song lyric or a paragraph from a book your teen is reading. Take turns: one person names one method precisely; the other must say its effect in a single sentence with the exact words quoted. No effect, no point.","keyIdea":"Strong analysis names the most precise method and then explains its effect. A semantic field is a group of words sharing one theme. A caesura is a pause inside a line of poetry; enjambment is a sentence running on past the end of a line. Sibilance is repeated hissing s, sh or z sounds. Juxtaposition places contrasting things side by side, foreshadowing hints early at later events, and a motif is an image or idea that recurs. Naming a term earns little on its own: say what it does to the reader, using the exact words as evidence.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each method work on words and sounds, or on the structure of the text? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Words and sounds","Lines and structure"],"items":[["semantic field",0],["sibilance",0],["connotation",0],["plosive alliteration",0],["caesura",1],["enjambment",1],["foreshadowing",1],["motif",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmethod","caption":"Flagship lab: find a semantic field, tag caesura and enjambment, sort sibilance, name structural methods and build an analysis.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
